p.src:
At this point the lecture makes its crucial turn. The attractive theory becomes dangerous when it is trusted too much. The financial crisis is narrated as a failure of the independence assumption. Portfolio theory had encouraged people to think that perhaps one investment might go bad, but not all of them together. The crisis showed that this was false. In the language above, the neglected covariance terms return precisely in the bad states: $\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j) &>0 \quad \text{in crisis states} \\
&\Longrightarrow \text{losses do not average out.}
\end{aligned}$

